# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Scyne stack configuration — THE one env file.
#
#   cp .env.example .env     # then fill in the four secrets below
#
# .env is gitignored; this template is not.
#
# Every process in the stack reads THIS file and no other: the orchestrator
# (orchestrator.config.ts), the chatbot server (scyne-chatbot/server/env.ts),
# the scripts, and `.mcp.json`'s ${MCP_TOKEN_FOR_AZURE}. There is no
# scyne-chatbot/.env any more — two copies of ADO_ORG and GEMINI_API_KEY
# drifted, and the PAT lived in only one of them, so the chatbot's
# approval-time ADO check reported "token present: false" while the same
# token published fine from the agents.
#
# `.env.local` is loaded after this one, for per-machine overrides. Neither
# file overwrites a variable that is already set, so `CHATBOT_PORT=4001 npm
# run dev` still beats the file.
#
# Uncommented below = what this install actually needs. Everything commented
# out has a working default in code — uncomment only to override it.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


# ─── Which CLI runs the agents ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# `codex` moves EVERY agent onto the Codex CLI. Per-agent, per-project and
# per-step overrides still win over this.
SCYNE_ADAPTER=codex

# Naming the model is what makes Codex runs COSTABLE.
#
# Codex reports token counts and no dollar figure, so a run is priced from the
# `model_prices` table — and a price needs a model name. The Codex transcript
# carries no model field (verified against a real capture), so the ONLY record
# of what a run was billed at is what the engine resolved. Leave this unset and
# Codex runs still work, but every one of them shows `—` instead of a cost and
# no cost budget can fire on them.
#
# It must be a model THIS account actually serves — naming one it does not is
# how an entire org's runs die on their first request. Current Codex models are
# gpt-5.6-sol / gpt-5.6-terra / gpt-5.6-luna; gpt-5.4 and gpt-5.4-mini retire
# from Codex on 2026-08-31. `scyne models list` shows the catalogue.
# The AZURE DEPLOYMENT name, because CODEX_BASE_URL points at an Azure
# endpoint — a Codex catalogue name like gpt-5.6-terra 404s there.
CODEX_MODEL=gpt-5.6-terra

# Where Codex runs go. Runs are spawned with --ignore-user-config, so
# ~/.codex/config.toml (which routes this machine through the local Azure
# proxy) never reaches them — without these, every run falls back to
# api.openai.com and 401s while interactive `codex` keeps working.
# The key is passed to Codex by VARIABLE NAME, never on argv.
CODEX_BASE_URL=
CODEX_API_KEY=

# Only read when SCYNE_ADAPTER=codex is NOT the whole story — an Azure Foundry
# adapter target. Unset here.
# AZURE_AI_PROJECT_ENDPOINT=
# AZURE_AI_API_KEY=
# AZURE_AI_MODEL=
# AZURE_AI_TOKEN=

# Codex wire-protocol overrides. Defaults suit the endpoint above.
# CODEX_PROVIDER_NAME=
# CODEX_WIRE_API=


# ─── Agent model auth — only when SCYNE_ADAPTER is a Claude adapter ─────────
# Neither is read by our code: they are inherited by the `claude` CLI the
# runner spawns. Set EXACTLY ONE, and only when running the claude_local
# adapter — this install runs Codex, so both stay off.
#
# Option A: pay-as-you-go API key.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Option B: use your Claude subscription (no API key). Generate a long-lived
# token on the host with:   claude setup-token
# (Mounting ~/.claude does NOT work on macOS — the login lives in the Keychain,
#  which the Linux container can't read. The token is the supported path.)
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=



# ─── Where deliverables are published ───────────────────────────────────────
# `atlassian` publishes documents to Confluence and the backlog to Jira.
# `ado` publishes to an Azure DevOps wiki and its work items.
#
# This decides it for a NEW project only. A project that has ALREADY published
# keeps the system it published into — the target is recorded in
# projects/<project>/.published.json and every publishing script reads it from
# there, because a client has links to those documents and splitting a pack
# across two systems is worse than either. `ensure-confluence-space.mjs`
# refuses loudly on a mismatch rather than letting a run do that.
PUBLISH_TARGET=atlassian

# Atlassian credentials. Publishing goes through the Atlassian MCP (see
# .mcp.json); these are for the two things it cannot do — ATTACHMENTS, because
# its OAuth grant has no attachment scope at all and a page publishes with its
# diagrams SILENTLY missing, and large page bodies, which must not travel
# through a model's context.
#
# Basic auth against the SITE domain. A token from
# https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens — an MCP OAuth
# token CANNOT be used here and fails looking like a permissions problem.
#
# Until these are set, publishing to Atlassian refuses with a named error
# rather than half-succeeding.
# ATLASSIAN_SITE_URL=https://your-site.atlassian.net
# ATLASSIAN_EMAIL=you@example.com
# ATLASSIAN_API_TOKEN=

# ─── Azure DevOps — the other publishing target ─────────────────────────────
# Used when PUBLISH_TARGET=ado, and by every project that already publishes
# there. Wiki pages instead of Confluence pages; work items instead of Jira
# issues.
#
# The PAT is MCP_TOKEN_FOR_AZURE (kept under its existing name — it already
# works, and renaming a live credential buys nothing). `.mcp.json` reads it as
# ${MCP_TOKEN_FOR_AZURE} rather than inlining it, because that file is
# committed and a PAT in it is a PAT in the git history. The chatbot's
# approval-time check reads the same variable (or ADO_PAT, if you prefer to
# separate them).
#
# The PAT needs BOTH scopes: vso.wiki_write and vso.work_write. Azure DevOps
# answers a MISSING SCOPE with 401 rather than 403, so a wiki 401 beside a
# working project call means a scope, not a bad token. Check with:
#   node scripts/ado-publish.mjs --verify
MCP_TOKEN_FOR_AZURE=
# ADO_PAT=

# One Azure DevOps ORGANISATION holds every project. The PROJECT is created per
# Scyne project by the New Project wizard (Agile template) and recorded in
# projects/<project>/.published.json under "adoTarget" — so there is deliberately
# no ADO_PROJECT and no ADO_WORK_ITEM_TYPE here. ONE target for the whole
# installation is exactly what per-project targets replaced, and a fallback to
# one would publish a client's document into another client's project.
ADO_ORG=

# Which wiki. Leave unset when the project has exactly one.
# ADO_WIKI=
# The work item every story is created under. Unset = none.
# ADO_PARENT_EPIC_ID=


# ─── Chatbot LLM (Gemini) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
GEMINI_API_KEY=
# Defaults to gemini-2.5-flash in llm.ts — set only to override.
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash
# GEMINI_TRANSCRIBE_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash
# GEMINI_LIVE_MODEL=models/gemini-2.5-flash-native-audio-latest


# ─── Chatbot workflow defaults ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# All off. Nothing here needs to be set for a run.
#
# DEFAULT_FEATURE_NAME is GONE, not merely commented: it was read by exactly
# one route (`/api/trigger`, the requirements stage) and by nothing else, so
# the feature display name in an issue title came from a global while every
# other stage derived it from the feature FOLDER. Set to a SADA-era label, it
# titled an RTWSA run `Generate requirements — Review & Verify Evidence
# (RTWSA/Appeals)` — a different client's feature, on the one stage that
# publishes a wiki page and creates the backlog. That route now derives the
# name the same way the others do.
#
# The three below are PARSED AND THEN DROPPED, and are off for that reason.
#
# They travel .env → the issue description → `issues.params` as processL3 /
# processL4 / startingStoryNumber, and stop. No workflow step interpolates any
# of them; the BA's generated prompt says only "your inputs are staged, invoke
# the skill". The skill's `## Required parameters` block does ask for them, but
# offers `inputs/metadata.yaml` as the way in and nothing writes that file —
# and skills/requirement-generator/SKILL.md then says the opposite anyway:
# "The process model carries the real L1/L2/L3 numbering; prefer it over
# inventing a process hierarchy." Since the capability map became a PROJECT
# stage, `process-model.json` is where story numbering actually comes from.
#
# Setting one does nothing but add a line to an issue description. Left here,
# commented, because that is a claim worth being able to re-check rather than
# a key worth deleting silently.
# DEFAULT_PROCESS_L3=2.4 Review & Verify evidence
# DEFAULT_PROCESS_L4=2.4.1 Review evidence
# DEFAULT_STARTING_STORY_NUMBER=2.4.1.1


# ─── Ports and paths — all derived, all overridable ─────────────────────────
# CHATBOT_PORT, not PORT: one shared env file feeds every process in the
# stack, and `PORT` is a name half the Node world reads. `PORT` is still
# honoured as a fallback, because Docker and most PaaS hosts inject it.
# Default 4000.
# CHATBOT_PORT=4000

# Where the chatbot reaches the orchestrator. Default http://127.0.0.1:3100.
# ORCHESTRATOR_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3100

# The workspace root — the directory holding projects/, skills/ and this file.
# BOTH processes derive it from their own install location, so it is only
# needed when the tree lives somewhere else (Docker sets /workspace). A value
# copied from another machine is logged and ignored rather than failing every
# write with EACCES.
# WORKSPACE_PATH=

# Server-side orchestrator token, for work belonging to no signed-in user (the
# staleness sweep). A run started from chat carries the USER'S token instead.
# SCYNE_API_TOKEN=

# Postgres instead of the bundled PGlite at .orchestrator/pgdata.
# DATABASE_URL=

# The two file-processing plugins, which are meant to run AT THE SAME TIME.
# Namespaced per plugin on purpose: they both used to default to 8080/8081 and
# to /tmp/afp, so installing both gave two servers fighting for one port and
# two workers overwriting each other's scratch. A shared ORCH_PORT would have
# moved both at once, which is why these are not called that.
# SCYNE_AWS_FILE_PORT=8080
# SCYNE_AWS_WORKSPACE_PORT=8081
# SCYNE_AWS_TEMP_DIR=/tmp/scyne-aws-files
# SCYNE_AZURE_FILE_PORT=8090
# SCYNE_AZURE_WORKSPACE_PORT=8091
# SCYNE_AZURE_TEMP_DIR=/tmp/scyne-azure-files

# ─── Where document BYTES live ───────────────────────────────────────────
# One store per stack, addressed by SHA-256 either way:
#
#   Claude -> AWS S3      (with Jira / Confluence)   see .env.claude
#   Codex  -> Azure Blob  (with Azure DevOps)        see .env.codex
#
# Unset both → in-memory, which does NOT survive the process. Fine for a test,
# never for a real run.
#
# BOTH may be set, and the two plugins are meant to run together. Reads then
# span every configured store, because a locator names its owner
# (`s3:<bucket>/…` vs `azure:<container>/…`), so nothing already written
# becomes unreadable. New bytes go to ONE store — SCYNE_BLOB_WRITE, defaulting
# to s3. The orchestrator prints which at boot.
#
# The SDKs are loaded on demand: a Claude install pulls in nothing from
# @azure/storage-blob, and a Codex install nothing from the AWS SDK.

# --- Claude / AWS.
# SCYNE_S3_ENDPOINT set → LocalStack or MinIO, and the well-known emulator
# credentials are used automatically for a loopback address. Unset it for real
# AWS: credentials then come from the SDK's default provider chain (profile,
# SSO, EC2/ECS/EKS role), which is why a production deployment needs no secret
# here at all.
# SCYNE_S3_DOCUMENTS_BUCKET=scyne-documents
# SCYNE_S3_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:4566
# SCYNE_S3_REGION=us-east-1
# SCYNE_S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true          # defaults true when an endpoint is set

# --- Codex / Azure.
# AZURE_STORAGE_CONNECTION_STRING=
# AZURE_DOCUMENTS_CONTAINER=documents     # default in orchestrator.config.ts

# Which store takes WRITES when both are configured. Default s3.
# SCYNE_BLOB_WRITE=s3

# `npm run migrate:blobs -- --apply` moves an install that still keeps bytes in
# Postgres. Run it BEFORE pointing at a store, or every existing document reads
# back absent.

# Where the chatbot serves its built frontend from, and which origin it
# accepts. Both derived in dev.
# STATIC_DIR=
# CHATBOT_ORIGIN=
